06 extract exactly dimensions, joint smoothness, |q|=1, unit constraint, pointwise existential normality without regularity of its scalar, and locally time-uniform compact support. H003 remains explicitly present and unused; no use of |q|=1 is needed once the constant-q support condition is given. Natural-number dimensions with H001 are the same positive integer dimensions. D001-D003 define the open domain and component derivatives/Laplacian; the zero extensions in D002 are only native total-function views, never smoothness claims across t=0. D004 preserves the exact energy density. D005 uses a total integral convention without assuming finiteness; N002 and R003/S004 prove it is the actual finite Lebesgue integral at every positive time. D004/D005 have H002 guards, available directly in each consuming inference. D006 fixes standard product Lebesgue normalization, including dimension zero, and D007 fixes the real dot product. Principal sources M001-M014 were actually probed in the pinned library and their defining source passages read: the selected M003 is the filter dominated-convergence theorem, and the selected M006 is integral_prod_symm (not the earlier exploratory declarations). All restatements include their native structures/premises: real/complex-like scalar restrictions for inner derivatives, minSmoothness for symmetric derivatives, open-domain smooth derivative loss, countably generated filter, finite-on-compacts measure, s-finite Fubini measures, complete FTC codomain, extended-norm finiteness and interval compactness structures. No external E is selected; the Evans quotation is task provenance, not a borrowed theorem. The graph has exactly R001→N001, R002→N002, R003→N003, R004→N004, R005→P, with every selected M and every N in P's AND closure. The route of problem.md:52-159 is preserved, including the explicit scalar equation comment, common compact box, mean-value bound, dominated-convergence limit, coordinatewise FTC/Fubini, cancellation, and constancy. No material proof passage was omitted. The current structural check is not mathematical certification; these are same-author findings after reading all five actual conditional packets.\",\"readiness\":\"no_known_issue\",\"open_issues\":[],\"resolutions\":[],\"output_notes\":[{\"id\":\"N001\",\"note\":\"Checked output against R001/S001-S003. Smoothness of derivatives and the open-domain interpretation support the product differentiations. F001 explicitly exports tangency throughout Ω before S002 differentiates it; S002 obtains the normal acceleration orthogonality without differentiating a and also derives its explicit value. S003 exports the full e_t formula with factor 2 and the mixed-partial identification, not merely a reference to an original equation.\"},{\"id\":\"N002\",\"note\":\"Checked the full quantified support/integrability interface against R002/S001-S004. The witnesses C,R are simultaneous for J=[t0/2,3t0/2], Q is the associated box, C⊂int Q, and zero derivatives are only asserted at interior times. F003 lists all later scalar integrands and derivatives, not merely e. S004 gives compact support, boundedness, absolute integrability and its finite volume bound for each. The output also explicitly exports integrability of every b1_Q, used by R003; this is not left private in F002. The arbitrary-time integrability conclusion is included.\"},{\"id\":\"N003\",\"note\":\"R003/S001-S004 proves differentiability of the actual E from the difference quotient limit, then the exact two-term integral formula. The window η=t0/4 remains inside J, the majorant B1_Q is integrable by N002, both signs of h are covered, and all displayed integrals are finite. No assertion about exchanging a limit or identifying E with a finite integral is merely assumed.\"},{\"id\":\"N004\",\"note\":\"R004/S001-S003 proves the exact spatial integration-by-parts identity for each t0>0 and each valid spatial index, with a minus sign on the second-derivative term. Both integrals' finiteness comes directly from N002. The interface does not depend on an unexported g or a private box: these are existentially chosen inside the proof and eliminated before universal export.\"},{\"id\":\"P\",\"note\":\"R005/S001 combines precisely N003's derivative formula, N004's negative spatial integrals, N002's finiteness and N001's zero pointwise normal work. It proves all three original clauses for every t>0. R005/S002 then derives constancy on positive times by M004, including equal and reversed endpoints. D004/D005 retain coefficients 1 on the two squares, and no derivative or value at t=0 is claimed.\"}],\"inference_notes\":[{\"id\":\"R001\",\"dependency_and_local_assumption_check\":\"Inspected the packet's H002,H004,H005,M001,M002,M009 and the actual proof S001-S003. H002 gives C∞ on Ω, M009 iterates derivative regularity, M002 supplies the needed time-space commutation, and M001 differentiates each inner product. The identity differentiated in S002 is genuinely a neighborhood identity: S001 and F001 explicitly establish u·u_t=0 throughout Ω. H005 supplies a pointwise scalar witness; finite dot-product algebra makes its normal work zero without any regularity of a. The two second derivatives of the unit constraint give the displayed value of any such scalar. S003 applies the same product rule to the density's finite sum and uses F001's mixed equality. No input merely duplicates an unexplained source invocation, no ancestor is used, and universal introduction over the arbitrary point leaves no private witness in the output.\",\"source_application_check\":\"Checked R001 applications A001-A005 against their native contracts. M009: K=ℝ, Ω open, C∞ from H002 and successive applications, with ∞+1=∞; evaluation on fixed coordinate vectors identifies the partials. M002: the current point has t>0, H002 gives C², and minSmoothness(ℝ,2)=2. M001 is used along real time or spatial lines into the Euclidean inner-product space ℝ^m; the first/second derivatives required for each factor are supplied by H002/F001. Its conclusion is exactly the sum of the two real inner products, whose symmetry produces the factors 2. No complex sesquilinear normalization is silently substituted.\",\"declaration_check\":\"Checked x,t introduced at S001 with scope through S003, and a chosen from direct H005 at S002, not from a later fact. The a-value is exported only as a conditional assertion for every scalar satisfying its equation. Source-bound coordinate vectors and indices are locally quantified by their uses, and y,r in F001 are statement-bound to the explicitly universal tangency statement. Ω, ∂, Δ_x, dot, e come from the packet's D definitions, with H002 discharging e's guard.\"},{\"id\":\"R002\",\"dependency_and_local_assumption_check\":\"Inspected S001-S004 against direct H001,H002,H006,N001 and the listed six analytic sources. H006 is instantiated only after 0<t0/2<3t0/2 is displayed; it provides a common C for J, not separately chosen supports for each time. M008 on the norm gives a finite radius even for empty C, and F001 precedes choice of R. S003 uses equality to the constant on an open complement and zero constant difference quotients repeatedly, rather than assuming support of derivatives. N001 supplies smoothness and continuity. Closed nonzero support lies in compact C; M007 gives integrability and M008 plus the integrable indicator gives the absolute-integral estimate. H002 also directly discharges the density-definition guard. Standard elementary closure facts for continuous/smooth finite sums, Euclidean closed compact sets, and monotonicity of integrals are used in their usual rigorous form; there is no extra H or hidden project lemma.\",\"source_application_check\":\"M008 in S001 is applied to |x| on the compact C from H006, so its ordered codomain is ℝ and its function is continuous. M014 in S002 uses the standard real finite-coordinate CompactIccSpace structure (the real, pi and product instances were read in Topology/Order/Compact.lean:77-91). M013 at endpoints ±R gives exactly (2R)^n, hence finite measure. M011 has finite real constant norm and finite measure Q; M012 has closed measurable Q and converts integrability-on-Q to the actual zero-extended majorant. M007 in S004 has the continuous sections and compact supports proved in F003 and Lebesgue finite-on-compacts. The second M008 application uses compact Q from F002 and the continuous absolute values from F003. Every stated native source condition has an actual local ground.\",\"declaration_check\":\"t0 is arbitrary and positive; J is its explicit interval; C is an H006 witness in S001. The radius existence F001 is established before R is chosen in S002. Q depends only on this same R. Section times r, spatial indices j and auxiliary g_j are explicitly quantified within S003/F003 and the output, with g_j's defining formula given. Bounds in S004 are existential for each scalar section, not a claimed uniform bound on an unbounded time interval. C,R,J,Q do not escape except through the quantified existential output.\"},{\"id\":\"R003\",\"dependency_and_local_assumption_check\":\"Inspected the packet using only direct H002,N001,N002 and M003,M004,M008,M014. N002's complete statement supplies matching support witnesses, compact finite box, all required integrability, and the integrable constant indicator. S001 chooses η=t0/4 and proves a compact positive-time box before obtaining B. S002 handles positive and negative h by the min/max interval and divides only by |h|>0; outside Q both densities are zero. The derivative definition in N001 supplies pointwise convergence of D_h. S003 checks the punctured real filter's countable basis and actually invokes dominated convergence. S004 uses linearity only on integrable functions to identify ∫D_h with the quotient of finite energies and concludes the derivative limit. This is not a bare differentiation-under-integral assertion. H002 is directly available for the e,E guards.\",\"source_application_check\":\"M014 is instantiated on Q×[t0−η,t0+η] as a real coordinate order interval. M008 uses its compactness and N001's continuous e_t on Ω, since t0−η>0. M004 takes f(r)=e(x,r), endpoints min/max(t0,t0+h), bound B from earlier F001, and z the upper endpoint; the interval is nondegenerate and contained in int J, so the within derivative equals the ordinary derivative, including at the lower endpoint. M003 has A=ℝ^n, G=ℝ, μ=dx, index h and the countably generated punctured filter at zero. F002 gives eventual measurability and pointwise domination by the integrable B1_Q; N001 gives the pointwise derivative limit. Its returned integral limit is exactly the limit recorded in F003 and then used to prove N003.\",\"declaration_check\":\"t0,C,R,J,Q,η are introduced in S001 with the matching N002 witness basis. Existence of B is F001; B is not chosen until S002. D_h is a total family: it is the actual quotient for 0<|h|<η and zero otherwise, so no undefined time or division is used in the source application. Spatial x, time r and increment h are locally bound in formulas; their associated statements F001-F003 record all domains. All box and bound objects remain live through S004 and are eliminated by arbitrary t0 export.\"},{\"id\":\"R004\",\"dependency_and_local_assumption_check\":\"Inspected S001-S003 against direct H001,H002,N001,N002,M001,M005,M006,M010. N001 supplies smooth factors; N002 supplies common support and finiteness of g, its derivative and both summands. M001 actually derives the derivative product formula. C⊂int Q makes the two endpoint faces zero; continuity plus finite interval gives interval integrability for the one-dimensional derivative. S002 then applies FTC before Fubini, not in the opposite logical order. The insertion equivalence identifies product measure with dx, and compact support makes the box indicator harmless. F002 is zero integral over Q; S003 extends to all space and rearranges two finite integrals. The n=1 case is explicitly the FTC case with a zero-dimensional remaining factor, not an unhandled induction base. H002 supplies the guarded density data included in N001/N002; no original unit or normality H is imported independently.\",\"source_application_check\":\"M001: real Euclidean targets and the two j-coordinate derivative premises are in N001. M005: its codomain ℝ is complete, g's sections are differentiable on all ℝ and their continuous derivatives are interval integrable on [−R,R]; its endpoint subtraction is zero by F001. M010: k=n−1 with n≥1 from H001, all A_i=ℝ, valid coordinate j−1 and sigma-finite coordinate Lebesgue measures. M006 is specifically integral_prod_symm; A is the selected coordinate and B is the remaining coordinate space, so the source conclusion performs the FTC integral first. Its product integrability is carried from F001 through the measure-preserving measurable equivalence M010; there is no scale factor. Standard interval-integrability of continuous real sections and transport of integrability/integrals by a measure-preserving measurable equivalence are ordinary basic library bridges with all their premises present.\",\"declaration_check\":\"t0 and j are arbitrary within the specified positive time/finite index domains. C and the matching R come from direct N002; Q is defined from R. g is explicitly defined in S001 and its smoothness follows in the same argument, not assumed by its name. The remaining coordinates in S002 are fixed arbitrary coordinates for the one-dimensional argument and integrated afterward. No such coordinate or private box is free in the exported N004.\"},{\"id\":\"R005\",\"dependency_and_local_assumption_check\":\"Inspected the final packet, not any producer body: N002 states integrability, N003 states differentiability and its complete derivative expression, N004 states exactly the negative spatial integral identity, and N001 states zero pointwise work. S001 substitutes these actual statements, uses finite linearity with the stated finiteness, and obtains all of F001. H002 is directly present for the guarded definitions. S002 covers ordered, equal and reversed positive endpoints; no temporary order assumption survives. M004 is used only after the derivative has been proved zero on the whole positive interval.\",\"source_application_check\":\"M004 in S002 is instantiated with V=ℝ, f=the zero extension of E to nonpositive times, a=s,b=t,z=t,C=0 for 0<s<t. F001 gives differentiability and zero ordinary derivative on a neighborhood of the entire interval, so the within derivative is also zero and the native inequality gives equality of endpoints. The extension cannot affect derivatives at positive times. The remaining branches use equality and order symmetry, not an extra constancy theorem.\",\"declaration_check\":\"All functions in S001 have the global D definitions; its t and j occur under the explicit universal and finite-sum bindings. S002 declares the arbitrary positive endpoints s,t as inference locals limited to that paragraph. There are no witnesses in the final packet, no boundary value at zero, and no free support set or bound. E is the finite integral by the directly stated N002, rather than by its total-convention definition alone.\"}] }","status":"completed"}],"error":null,"incomplete_details":null,"tools":[{"type":"function","name":"search_mathlib","description":"在固定 mathlib 的 Lean 源码中搜索正则表达式，返回至多 100 行。","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_mathlib","description":"读取固定 mathlib 中一个 Mathlib/...lean 文件的指定行。","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"path":{"type":"string"},"start_line":{"type":"integer"},"line_count":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["path","start_line","line_count"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"search_external_sources","description":"Follow source-retrieval.md: arxiv first (default; an exact arXiv ID is allowed), then google title/author search, then google title/author DOI search, then scihub with a confirmed DOI. Use browser:true for normal local-browser readback of JavaScript/verification pages. Results and DOI strings are locators, not source/theorem approval. Record failed access and continue conditionally when the original stays unavailable.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string"},"engine":{"type":"string","enum":["arxiv","google","scihub"]},"browser":{"type":"boolean"}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_external_source","description":"Read and retain a public HTTPS source and its hash-bound access record. Use offset to continue cached text. Retry with browser:true for a normal isolated local Chrome session if HTTPS needs JavaScript or verification. The optional browser uses ordinary site controls; unsupported challenges remain explicit failures. Verify full PDF identity, version, theorem and context yourself.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"url":{"type":"string"},"offset":{"type":"integer"},"limit":{"type":"integer"},"browser":{"type":"boolean"}},"required":["url"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"defer_primary_source","description":"After actual unavailable primary-source access, preserve a complete exact secondary quotation as an explicitly unverified external assumption. The statement must match the quote in a supplied input. Returns the verification record for E.source.verification; this never grants ordinary Audit A PASS.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"source_id":{"type":"string"},"statement":{"type":"string"},"input_id":{"type":"string"},"quote":{"type":"string"},"attempts":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},"required":["source_id","statement","input_id","quote","attempts"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_task_input","description":"Read one declared frozen document. For JSON use pointer (empty string lists the root); large objects return keys, and strings resume with offset=next_offset. For text use inclusive start/end lines and column=next_column when continuing a long line. Every serialized result is at most 32 KiB. Follow has_more and the returned continuation coordinates; no arbitrary local files are accessible.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"start":{"type":"integer"},"end":{"type":"integer"},"column":{"type":"integer"},"pointer":{"type":"string"},"offset":{"type":"integer"},"limit":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["id"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_yui","description":"Read declared Yui rules/templates; no tests, historical runs or personal files.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"path":{"type":"string"},"start":{"type":"integer"},"end":{"type":"integer"},"column":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["path","start","end"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"check_artifact","description":"Check a JSON artifact against the explicit stage contract; this is not automatic mathematical certification.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact_json":{"type":"string"}},"required":["artifact_json"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"submit_artifact","description":"Submit a JSON artifact after its stage checks.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact_json":{"type":"string"}},"required":["artifact_json"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"report_obstacle","description":"Report only a real substantive/suspected mathematical GAP, unavailable source or conflicting original input, with all five located fields. Ordinary unfinished NL0, source registration and JSON bookkeeping are not reports: continue an actual lookup or draft edit. TASK_INCOMPLETE is unavailable for NL0 authoring. Difficulty or a long run is not evidence of a mathematical gap.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"status":{"type":"string","enum":["SUBSTANTIVE_GAP","SUSPECTED_GAP","SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE","MISSING_SOURCE_STATEMENT","INPUT_CONFLICT"]},"location":{"type":"string"},"explanation":{"type":"string"},"basis":{"type":"string"},"partial_progress":{"type":"string"}},"required":["status","location","explanation","basis","partial_progress"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"check_draft","description":"Preferred structured-object form of check_artifact: provide artifact as an actual JSON object, not an escaped JSON string. Same complete validation and acceptance scope.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact":{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true}},"required":["artifact"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"submit_draft","description":"Preferred structured-object form of submit_artifact: provide artifact as an actual JSON object, not an escaped JSON string. Same complete validation and acceptance scope.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact":{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true}},"required":["artifact"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"probe_library","description":"Query a proved Mathlib theorem and its exact signature/axioms in the offline sandbox. Returns located evidence for an M source; the mathematical statement and applicability still require review. The optional module locator uses dotted syntax (Mathlib.Topology.Basic), never a slash path; omit it if unknown.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"declaration":{"type":"string"},"module":{"type":"string"}},"required":["id","declaration"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"find_library_sources","description":"Search pinned foundational metadata by an exact declaration name or ordinary mathematical terms. Ranked lexical candidates are not a theorem whitelist or an applicability judgment. Selected sources still need explicit M roots and complete checks.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_draft","description":"Read a JSON pointer in your own same-stage candidate after an actual structural check. No other file/stage is accessible. For an existing string optionally supply both start_line/end_line to request up to 250 numbered lines and its complete text_sha256 for replace_lines. A read end beyond the text returns the remaining existing lines and actual range; the start must exist. Edits never clamp their range.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"candidate":{"type":"integer"},"pointer":{"type":"string"},"start_line":{"type":"integer"},"end_line":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["candidate","pointer"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_author_packet","description":"Same-author closing inspection of one structurally valid own conditional inference: exact direct inputs, output, declarations and own proof. No ancestor bodies, extra H, independent reviewer or Audit A feedback.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"candidate":{"type":"integer"},"inference":{"type":"string"}},"required":["candidate","inference"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"record_author_checks","description":"Save actual same-author notes and readiness against shared Audit A criteria. Unresolved/not-checked partial notes can be saved, but only complete actual packet inspection with no known issue can release a proof. Open issues persist until explicit located resolutions. No PASS or mathematical verdict is generated; independent Audit A receives none of these notes.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"candidate":{"type":"integer"},"original_context_note":{"type":"string"},"readiness":{"type":"string","enum":["no_known_issue","unresolved","not_checked"]},"open_issues":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"criterion":{"type":"string"},"location":{"type":"string"},"note":{"type":"string"}},"required":["id","criterion","location","note"],"additionalProperties":false}},"resolutions":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"location":{"type":"string"},"note":{"type":"string"}},"required":["id","location","note"],"additionalProperties":false}},"output_notes":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"note":{"type":"string"}},"required":["id","note"],"additionalProperties":false}},"inference_notes":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"dependency_and_local_assumption_check":{"type":"string"},"source_application_check":{"type":"string"},"declaration_check":{"type":"string"}},"required":["id","dependency_and_local_assumption_check","source_application_check","declaration_check"],"additionalProperties":false}}},"required":["candidate","readiness","open_issues","original_context_note","output_notes","inference_notes"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false}],"tool_choice":"auto","parallel_tool_calls":false,"prompt_cache_key":"bb24fd3b-7dfc-44c5-b543-6a4c73316aea","prompt_cache_retention":"24h","text":{"format":{"type":"text"},"verbosity":"high"},"top_p":0.98,"top_logprobs":0,"temperature":1.0,"reasoning":{"effort":"high","context":"all_turns","summary":null,"mode":"standard"},"user":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":140248,"in